Q: How should I prevent duplicate processing of events in a distributed system? A: <p>Design for at-least-once and make processing idempotent. That's the answer to your last question, and it determines the rest.</p>
<p>Kafka does have exactly-once semantics, but that only holds when the sink is Kafka. Your sink is a payment, so the guarantee stops at the broker boundary and you still need idempotent processing. Also worth noting you don't say which broker you're on, and most of them (SQS, RabbitMQ, Pub/Sub) offer nothing like this.</p>
<p>Also, put dedup in the database that owns the business state, in the same transaction as the effect:</p>
<pre><code>begin;
  insert into processed_events (event_id) values ('evt_12345')
  on conflict do nothing;
  -- 0 rows affected means already handled, roll back and ack
  update accounts set balance = balance + 1000 where user_id = 'user_42';
commit;
</code></pre>
<p>you can read more about producer delivery here: <a href="https://docs.confluent.io/kafka/design/delivery-semantics.html#producer-delivery" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.confluent.io/kafka/design/delivery-semantics.html#producer-delivery</a></p>
